[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: System Requirements
UID: DOC-SYSTEM

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: hardware.sgra

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Testable system-level requirements. Each one Refines a ``VIS-`` entry and is
itself refined by the discipline requirements in the ``20-`` documents.

The efficiency requirement ``SYS-004`` is the budget parent for the whole
design: the 300 W it allows at full power is divided among the discipline
requirements, and those ``BUDGET`` fields must sum back to it.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-001
TITLE: Maximum power delivery
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall deliver 3.0 kW into the vehicle pack at a coil-to-coil gap of
20 mm, measured at the pack terminals.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The programme target from the v1 white paper, retained because the vehicle pack
is expected to grow to roughly 8 kWh, at which point 3 kW is a 0.38C charge
completing in 2.7 h. Measured at the pack terminals rather than at the inverter
so that the number cannot be met by moving losses downstream.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
BUDGET: 3.0 kW
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-003
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-002
TITLE: Commandable charge rate
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall accept a commanded output current setpoint anywhere from 0 A to
the maximum permitted by ``SYS-001``, and shall hold it within 5% of setpoint in
steady state.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Today's pack is 48 V, 33 Ah - 1.58 kWh - and 3 kW into it is a 1.89C charge,
above what most cells accept. The pack, not the charger, is the present limit,
so the charger must be able to run well below its maximum on command. 5% is
chosen because it is comfortably inside the tolerance of any pack charge
profile while being achievable with the phase-shift resolution available.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-003
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-003
TITLE: Vehicle-side output voltage
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall deliver into a 48 V nominal pack over an operating range of
40 V to 60 V, and shall not require modification for any pack capacity.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Stated in the vision interview: the pack voltage is fixed for the life of the
programme and only capacity grows. The 40 V to 60 V window covers a 13S to 15S
lithium pack from deep discharge to full charge with margin. Capacity
independence means the design is sized by current and voltage, never by amp
hours.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-004
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-004
TITLE: End-to-end efficiency at full power
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall convert at least 90% of the power drawn from the dock bus into
power delivered at the pack terminals, at 3.0 kW and a 20 mm gap, i.e. total
losses shall not exceed 300 W.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is a thermal requirement expressed as an efficiency one. ``VIS-006`` allows
no pumped coolant and no forced air, so every watt lost has to conduct into the
structure. 300 W is what a bolted conduction path can plausibly be designed for
across the dock and vehicle together; it is not an aspiration but the ceiling
the cooling method sets. The 300 W is divided among ``ELE-001`` at 25 W,
``MEC-001`` at 120 W, ``ELE-002`` at 40 W, ``ELE-003`` at 100 W and ``ELE-004``
at 15 W, which sum exactly to the parent.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
BUDGET: 300 W
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-007
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-005
TITLE: No galvanic path between dock and vehicle
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall transfer power with no electrical conductor crossing between
dock and vehicle, and shall withstand full immersion of both coil assemblies in
seawater without a conductive path forming.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The reason the system is wireless at all. The immersion clause is what
distinguishes this from a dry inductive charger: the gap medium may be
conductive seawater rather than air, which affects both losses and safety.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-002
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-006
TITLE: Coupling at the docked position
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall meet ``SYS-001`` and ``SYS-004`` at any coil-to-coil gap from
5 mm to 20 mm with lateral misalignment up to 10 mm and angular misalignment up
to 3 degrees.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The vision interview states the dock holds the vehicle close and square. The
misalignment allowances are what a mechanically registered dock should achieve
without a precision mechanism; they exist so the magnetics are designed for a
range rather than for one nominal position that will never occur twice. The
resulting coupling coefficient is expected to be about 0.5 and is confirmed by
``MEC-003``.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-005
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-007
TITLE: Thermal rejection by conduction only
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall reject all losses by conduction into the dock and vehicle
structure, with no component exceeding its rated maximum operating temperature
at 3.0 kW continuous in the maximum specified ambient.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Stated in the vision interview. The ambient is still an open question and must
be answered before this requirement can be verified; it is recorded here so the
gap is visible rather than assumed away.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-006
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-008
TITLE: Fault detection and safe state
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall detect over-current, over-voltage, over-temperature and loss of
coupling, and shall reach a state in which no switching device conducts, within
10 microseconds of an over-current event and within 100 ms of the others.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A charger that destroys itself does not return the Mako to operation, which is
what ``VIS-001`` asks for; the word "safely" in that entry is the hook. The
10 microsecond figure is set by what a hardware comparator into the timer break
input can achieve, which is the only mechanism fast enough to save a bridge from
a shoot-through or a shorted secondary; the slower faults can be handled in the
control loop. Note that no vision entry covers safety directly - this was not
asked about in the interview and should be confirmed with the human.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-001
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-009
TITLE: Resonance tracking across the operating band
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall locate and hold the resonant frequency of the coupled tank
anywhere in an 80 kHz to 120 kHz band, and shall re-acquire within 2 s of a step
change in coupling.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Keeps the one thing v1 bench testing actually validated. The band is the white
paper's own, and 85 kHz sits inside it as the industry nominal. The 2 s
re-acquisition is set by how quickly a docking transient must be ridden through
without tripping ``SYS-008``.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-008
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-010
TITLE: Every rating traced to a primary source
STATEMENT: >>>
No component shall appear in a released BOM unless its voltage, current and
thermal ratings are recorded in ``docs/reference/manifest.yaml`` against a
fetched datasheet revision or a measurement made in this programme.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The v1 review found six part numbers that do not name orderable parts, a power
stage quoted at its absolute-maximum peak as though that were a continuous
rating, and a rectifier described as Schottky that is an ultrafast silicon
diode. Individually small; together they are why v1 never reached its target.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-010
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-011
TITLE: Manufacturable without specialist tooling
STATEMENT: >>>
Every board shall be fabricable to a standard 6-layer 2 oz capability class and
assembled with standard SMT processes, and every mechanical part shall be
producible without tooling specific to this programme, with the sole permitted
exception of the coil conductor if ``MEC-001`` cannot otherwise be met.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The v1 white paper chose PCB coils substantially for this reason and the
reasoning is sound. The analysis says the coil is likely to have to break this
rule to meet the loss budget; nothing else should. Naming the exception here is
what keeps it a single deliberate concession rather than a precedent.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-009
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-012
TITLE: Bidirectional in-band data link
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall carry bidirectional data over the coil pair at at least 200 bit
per second with no power flowing, and at least 2 kbit per second at any
throughput above 300 W, with per-frame error detection and a delivery latency
below 100 ms at rates above 300 W.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The two rates exist because the tank is a bandpass filter whose bandwidth is set
by its loaded quality factor, and the load swings that by a factor of fifty. At
3 kW the reflected load damps the tank to a loaded Q of about 2 and the
bandwidth is 43 kHz; with no load the tank returns to its unloaded Q of about 98
and the bandwidth collapses to 867 Hz, about 434 baud. Handshake happens at no
load, which is precisely the narrow case, so the two figures are separated
rather than averaged. 200 bit per second carries a 16-byte handshake frame in
under a second; 2 kbit per second is ample for telemetry and setpoint updates
once power is flowing. See ``docs/design/in-band-comms.md``.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-011
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-013
TITLE: No power without a completed handshake
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall not raise the transmitter drive above the handshake level until
it has exchanged and validated an identification frame with a receiver, and
shall return to that level within 500 ms of losing the link.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This is what makes the communication link a safety feature rather than a
convenience. Without it the dock energises 3 kW into whatever is in front of it,
which may be a foreign object, a flooded receiver or nothing at all. Tying the
interlock to the link means the receiver has to be present, identified and
answering before any real power flows, and the same mechanism gives
loss-of-coupling detection for free. 500 ms is short enough that an undocking
transient cannot leave the dock driving into open air for long, and long enough
not to trip on a single lost frame.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-011
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-014
TITLE: Charge rate is a C-rate against configured pack capacity
STATEMENT: >>>
The system shall charge at 0.3C to 0.5C of the configured pack capacity in
normal operation, shall never exceed 1.0C, and shall derive that limit from a
configured capacity rather than from a fixed current.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Stated in the vision interview. The consequence is worth writing down plainly:
today's 48 V, 33 Ah pack is 1.58 kWh, so 1.0C is 1584 W and the usable ceiling
is 53% of the charger's 3 kW rating; normal charging is 475 W to 792 W. The
charger will therefore run well below its rating for the foreseeable life of
the present pack, which is the intended consequence of sizing for growth.

Working the same arithmetic the other way gives the pack this design point
assumes: 3 kW is a normal-band charge for a pack of 6 kWh at 0.5C to 10 kWh at
0.3C. The white paper's 8 kWh pack takes 3 kW at 0.375C over 2.7 h, squarely
inside the band - so the design point and the paper agree once the C-rate policy
is applied.

The limit must be a C-rate against a configured capacity, not a fixed current
limit, because the pack is explicitly expected to grow. A fixed current stops
protecting the pack the moment the capacity changes, and stops permitting full
power the moment it grows. See ``sim/link-budget/charge_policy.py``.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
BUDGET: 1.0 C maximum
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-003
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-015
TITLE: Bidirectional converter deployed in both orientations
STATEMENT: >>>
The dual active bridge shall transfer power in both directions at full rating,
and one design shall satisfy both the Leviathan-side step-up instance and the
Mako-side step-down instance without modification.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Implements ``VIS-012``. Two consequences follow that a unidirectional design
would not carry. First, the low-voltage bridge is a synchronous bridge in both
instances, since it rectifies in one direction and inverts in the other - which
also removes the diode-conduction loss a passive low-voltage rectifier would
cost at 62.5 A. Second, the low-voltage specification is the envelope of both
packs, so the Leviathan pack voltage is now a required input to the design and
is not yet known.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-012
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: SYS-016
TITLE: Environmental envelope for open-deck marine service
STATEMENT: >>>
The dock equipment shall operate to full rating over an ambient range of
-25 degC to +55 degC in an enclosure on the open deck of a surface vessel, and
shall survive storage from -30 degC to +70 degC.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The vessel operates worldwide, so the envelope comes from the marine standards
rather than from a site. IEC 60945 derives its exposed-equipment maximum as a
maximum expected air temperature at sea of +32 degC plus a maximum solar gain of
+23 degC, giving +55 degC. The solar term is the larger half, which is why an
open-deck enclosure and an enclosed space are not the same problem.

The -25 degC cold limit is the figure generally cited for the exposed class but
could not be confirmed against the standard itself, which is paywalled; it is
recorded blocked in ``docs/reference/manifest.yaml`` and needs a purchased copy
before anything is qualified against it. The +55 degC maximum is well attested
and is safe to design against now.

The consequence is the tight one: at +55 degC ambient with conduction cooling
only, and a 100 degC conductor limit in ``MEC-004``, the entire thermal chain
from coil conductor through ferrite, interface, cold plate and structure has a
45 degC budget. That is what sets the coil loss allowance in ``MEC-001``.
See ``docs/design/environment.md``.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: VIS-006
  ROLE: Refines
